     1	# Chapter 39 — Two Hundred Years
     2	
     3	Karis had five nights, and she used all of them, and on the Second-day she said she was ready.
     4	
     5	Nobody saw her work; that was the arrangement. The assistant went home at the sixth bell every evening with a story about glue, and believed it, and told Ephram, who told the refectory, so that by the Fourth-day half the hill knew that the researcher at the top of the library was mending a founding volume with something that smelled, and kept away. The lamp burned in the window at its usual height every night and went out at its usual hour. Behind it, at the far end of the long table under the brown-paper shade, Karis sat with the case open and the two keys in the room, Seln's for an hour at a time while he sat silent at the middle of the bench, and laid the whole of it out again, and again, and again, until it would lie in the order she wanted.
     6	
     7	Cael went up on the third night only to bring her bread, because Brom said somebody had to and Brom's shoulder did not like the top stair yet. She did not look up when he came in. The forty pairs were not in four rows any longer. They lay in a single long line down the whole of the table, sorted by something he could not see, with a slip of paper on each in her small upright hand. The memoranda stood beside them in nineteen separate piles, one issue to a pile. Every seal on every one of them had a numbered slip tucked under it, and the nine new leaves from Greyvane lay open across the window end in their fresh grey wax. Seln sat at the middle of the bench with his hands flat and watched her. Karis asked Cael one question without looking up, about Denvash, and he answered it standing at her elbow, and Seln said nothing at all.
     8	
     9	He put the bread down at her elbow and went away again. The next night, when Karis came down to the east hall with the rest of them, she brought the plate back empty, and he did not know whether she had eaten the bread or Seln had.
    10	
    11	On the Second-day after supper they came up as they came up every evening of the year, by their three ways, a few minutes apart. The assistant had gone home singing, and the lamp was in the window.
    12	
    13	Karis had put everything away.
    14	
    15	The case stood shut between the grey boxes on its shelf. The long table was bare again to its faded green cloth. In the middle of the cloth, under the shaded lamp, lay four slips of paper folded once and numbered on the outside in her hand, one to four, in a row. A little apart from them lay a fifth, face down.
    16	
    17	She waited until Seln had looked at the room in his order and come in and sat down, and Brom had lit the kettle's blue flame under the pot, and Lira had got her foot up on the stool. Then she put her hands flat on the table either side of the slips, the way Seln did, and Cael wondered if she knew she was doing it.
    18	
    19	"Four findings," she said, "and one limit. I'm going to give you them in the order I found them, which is the order they stand on each other in. I'm not going past the case. If anybody asks me to go past it, I'll stop, and I'll stop for good, and I mean that." She looked round at them. "Not because I'm cross, but because the minute I guess at something in front of this paper, the paper's no use to anybody, including me."
    20	
    21	"Nobody's going to ask," said Lira.
    22	
    23	"You will," said Karis, "all of you, because it'll be the most natural thing in the world. I'm telling you now so you'll know what I'm doing when I don't answer."
    24	
    25	She unfolded the first slip.
    26	
    27	---
    28	
    29	"The orders don't correct strength," she said. "They correct reach."
    30	
    31	She did not have the forty out. She did not need them. She had them by heart, every station and every margin, and she gave them in the order she had sorted them into, which was not the order Seln had laid them on the table.
    32	
    33	"I sorted them by the margin," she said, "not by the station or the date, but by what the keeper wrote at the edge of the reading, that the order told the clerk not to enter. And every margin is the same kind of thing. *Reads into. Something of. A second family, faint.* Not one keeper in forty wrote at the margin that a child was too strong. Several of the forty were weak; a first rank, and a low one, with a second family showing faintly behind it like a face behind a window. They were corrected all the same. And I went through every pair looking for one where the order took strength away and left the rest, and there isn't one. When a rank goes, it goes because the second family went, and the second family was what was holding the rank up." She moved one finger on the cloth. "They don't correct how much. They correct how far."
    34	
    35	"Reach," said Brom.
    36	
    37	"Reach, how far a reading goes past the one column the station has for it." She looked at the four slips. "Strength they leave alone, because strength they like. Strength's easy; you write it down in a rank and build somebody a ladder. Daeva was read at thirteen, in a garden court, and nobody ever corrected a line of hers. She's the strongest thing on the continent at her age, and they've never once taken anything off her paper. They built her a ladder to her height and called it a cycle."
    38	
    39	Cael sat very still.
    40	
    41	*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness.* He had the letter by heart; it lived inside the back board of Hesk's book, in the satchel across his chest. He had never told Karis its words. She had come to the same sentence from the far side of a case.
    42	
    43	"And reach is what they can't have," Karis went on, "because reach is what doesn't fit a column. A child who reads Current and something of Mire isn't a better Current. She's a question the registry hasn't got a box for. So the function closes the question before anybody asks it. Margin reading not to be entered. And she goes home with one family on her paper and grows into the paper, because people do."
    44	
    45	"Nine weeks of nothing," said Lira quietly.
    46	
    47	"Nine weeks of nothing. They watch to see whether the margin comes back, and it never does, not in forty. Of course it doesn't, when nobody's told her it was there."
    48	
    49	She turned the first slip over. There was more on the back.
    50	
    51	"The second half of the first finding is the mark on Cael's file."
    52	
    53	Nobody moved.
    54	
    55	"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
    56	
    57	"Advisory," said Seln.
    58	
    59	"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
    60	
    61	Cael had not known she had asked for anything but the bread. He remembered telling her, standing at her elbow with the plate in his hand, and her not looking up.
    62	
    63	"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
    64	
    65	She was quiet a moment.
    66	
    67	"She just never knew what was answering."
    68	
    69	Nobody said anything. Brom's kettle ticked on its shelf.
    70	
    71	"Ilsev did everything right," said Karis. "She's the most honest seat in the district. And Havel at the junior chair, who looks at the file every time it's on a table, as Brom's counted. He's seen something he can't place, and he's kept looking at it, which is all an honest man can do with a thing like that." She looked at each of them. "I want a rule about them before we go on."
    72	
    73	"Say it," said Lira.
    74	
    75	"Ilsev and Havel are never told. It would only cost them."
    76	
    77	Eleven words. Cael counted them because he could not help counting them, and found that he was glad there were so few.
    78	
    79	"Agreed," said Brom.
    80	
    81	"Agreed," said Lira.
    82	
    83	Seln moved one finger. "Agreed."
    84	
    85	"Agreed," said Cael.
    86	
    87	Karis nodded once, and wrote the eleven words on the back of the first slip, under the rest, and folded it again.
