   187	It went on past the point where silences end in rooms, past the point where somebody clears his throat or shifts in a chair or says a thing about the weather to let the room breathe. Nobody did any of those things. They had agreed, on the Third-day, without saying it, that whatever he did in the room would be his and nobody would help him with it.
   188	
   189	The clock on the landing ticked. Through the floor came the sound of the weight on the boards again below, and somebody laughing, and a door. The singer on the floor above had stopped, and Cael did not know when. Out of the window, across the slate roofs, the green cupola of the wool exchange stood in the grey afternoon, four streets off, with a thin smoke going up behind it from somebody's chimney in a straight line, because there was no wind. Brom breathed, slow and even, the way he breathed in a long hold at the frames. The cold stove ticked once, the way iron does when the day changes round it. Cael's ribs, which the healer had called mended a week ago, reminded him where they had been.
   190	
